# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generated build output. None of this belongs in the repository; all of it is
# reproducible from the sources plus the toolchain.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Dart / Flutter
.dart_tool/
build/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# Android
android/.gradle/
android/app/build/
android/local.properties        # machine-specific: SDK and Flutter paths
android/key.properties         # signing secrets
*.jks
*.keystore

# The compiled engine: libalex_store_native.so, which Gradle packages from
# jniLibs/ into the APK. A 7 MB build artifact, so it never belongs in the tree.
android/app/src/main/jniLibs/

# Rust
rust/target/

# Generated by `flutter_rust_bridge_codegen generate`, and deliberately not
# committed. The bindings are tied to the exact flutter_rust_bridge version
# pinned in rust/Cargo.toml (=2.13.0) and to the matching pubspec dependency: a
# tree generated by a different version compiles against the wrong runtime and
# fails at link time, not build time. `scripts/build-apk.mjs` regenerates it, so
# a clean checkout still produces a working APK.
lib/src/api/

# NOTE: `rust/.cargo/` is deliberately NOT ignored. That directory holds
# config.toml, which is real project configuration — the NDK linkers and the
# wasm32 +simd128 flag. Ignoring it is an easy mistake because rustup also
# writes a `.cargo` directory elsewhere; they are unrelated.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Editors and OS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.iws
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Model weights — fetched, not committed
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `rust/src/assets/model.bin` (7.1 MB) is a BUILD ARTIFACT and is not
# committed. Recover it from the published engine with:
#
#     npm install && node scripts/extract-model-from-wasm.mjs
#
# which replays the WASM's data segments into a memory image and pulls the model
# out of LINEAR MEMORY. Slicing the .wasm file does not work — see MEMORY.md
# §3 — and the result still loads and still returns unit-length vectors, so that
# failure is silent. The script locates the model by its own SHA-256, which makes
# the extraction self-verifying.
#
# Why it is not committed, when the rest of the vendored code is:
#
#   - **Size, and it is permanent.** Git history never forgets. The Store
#     repo's MEMORY.md §3 makes this exact argument about the APK: "an APK
#     committed to the repo is permanent in git history. Releases assets are
#     outside the tree, so they cost nothing."
#   - **Upstream does not commit it either.** ternlight's own `engine/assets/`
#     holds `.gitkeep` and `tokenizer.json`, and the README says model.bin is a
#     "release artifact, not committed". Matching upstream's convention is what
#     makes a future re-vendor a clean operation.
#   - **It is verifiable.** A checksum in the repo is stronger than a blob in
#     the repo — it also catches a corrupted *download*, which committing
#     cannot.
#
# `rust/src/assets/tokenizer.json` (694 KB) IS committed. It is upstream
# *source*, not an artifact: ternlight keeps it in git, and it is part of what
# "vendored byte-for-byte" means. The line is source vs artifact, not size.
#
rust/src/assets/model.bin

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lockfiles ARE committed
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The convention for libraries is to ignore pubspec.lock and Cargo.lock; this is
# an application, and both ecosystems recommend committing them so every build
# resolves identical dependency versions. For this project it is not a nicety:
# `flutter_rust_bridge`'s Dart version, the `flutter_rust_bridge_codegen` Rust
# version, and `flutter_rust_bridge` in rust/Cargo.toml must all agree, and a
# drift between them produces bindings that compile against the wrong runtime.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.env
.env.*
# npm is a dev-time convenience only, for the fixture and model scripts
# (scripts/generate-wasm-fixture.mjs, scripts/extract-model-from-wasm.mjs,
# scripts/reference-forward.mjs). Neither the Dart nor the Rust build reads
# node_modules. package.json and package-lock.json ARE committed so those
# scripts stay reproducible.
node_modules/

# Kotlin daemon logs and caches. android/gradle.properties disables
# incremental Kotlin because this project path contains spaces and the
# daemon cannot write its caches reliably there; these are its leftovers.
android/.kotlin/
